===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|alas}}, from {{der|en|fro|a las}} (French {{m|fr|hélas}}), from {{m|fro|a||ah}} + {{m|fro|las}}, from {{der|en|la|lassus||weary}}. Compare {{cog|nl|helaas}}, {{cog|frr|ielas}}, {{cog|fy|eilaas}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{q|obsolete}} {{l|en|helas}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/əˈlæs/|/əˈlɑːs/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/əˈlæs/}}
* {{a|NZ}} {{IPA|en|/ɘˈlɛs/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-alas.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-alas.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|æs|s=2}}

====Interjection====
{{en-interj}}

# {{ngd|Used to [[express]] [[sorrow]], [[regret]], [[compassion]] or [[grief]].}}
#: {{ux|en|I wanted to catch the last bus home, but '''alas''', I was ten minutes late and had to take a taxi instead.}}
#: {{syn|en|alack}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1521|author=w:John Skelton|title=Speke Parott
|passage='''Helas''' I lamente the dull abuſyd brayne<br>The enfatuate fantaſies the wytles wylfulnes<br>Of on and hothyr at me that haue dyſdayne}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|act=V|scene=i|page=278|column=1|passage='''Alas''' poore ''Yorick'', I knew him ''Horatio'', a fellow of infinite Ieſt; of moſt excellent fancy, he hath borne me on his backe a thouſand times: And how abhorred my Imagination is, my gorge riſes at it.}}

